-- title:
--   Common unit with simple zero and residue order tables
-- statement:
--   Throughout, $q$ is a prime, $N\ge 1$, and $A$ is a valuation subring of $\overline{\mathbb{Q}}$ whose residue field $\kappa:=$ `ResidueField A` has characteristic $q$ and is algebraically closed. Write $\bar F:=$ `modularFunctionFieldBar (N * q)` for the base change to $\overline{\mathbb{Q}}$ of the full modular function field of level $Nq$ inside Laurent series, $F_\kappa:=$ `modularFunctionFieldC (ResidueField A) N` for the subfield of $\kappa((q))$ generated by $j$ and $j_N$, and $F_\kappa^{\mathrm{full}}:=$ `modularFunctionFieldFullC (ResidueField A) N` for the field generated over $\kappa$ by the divisor expansions at level $N$. Places are `Place`s in the sense of the project: valuation subrings containing the image of the base field, proper, with principal ideal ring. The datum `data : ModularPolynomialData q` is a monic $\Phi$ in $\mathbb{Z}[X][Y]$ of degree $\psi(q)$ annihilating the pair $(j,j_q)$, and `hKr` is the Kronecker congruence, i.e. the coefficientwise reduction of $\Phi$ modulo $q$ equals $(\tilde X^{q}-X)(\tilde X-X^{q})$, $X$ denoting the outer and $\tilde X$ the inner variable. The hypotheses $h\alpha$, $h\beta$ assert that the two Hecke degeneracy embeddings `heckeAlphaBar` (the inclusion coming from $N \mid Nq$) and `heckeBetaBar` (the one given by $q$-expansion) from level $N$ into level $Nq$ over $\overline{\mathbb{Q}}$ are integral ring homomorphisms. Furthermore $P$ is a `PlaceSpecialization A q N data hKr` over $\kappa$ with reduction the residue map of $A$: a map `sp` from places of the level-$N$ field over $\overline{\mathbb{Q}}$ to places of $F_\kappa$, a homomorphism `spPic0` on degree-zero divisor classes, and the compatibility clauses of that structure relating orders of $j$, $j_N$ and of their differences from elements of $A$ upstairs with the corresponding orders downstairs. Its two reduction maps send a place $V$ of $\bar F$ to $\mathrm{reduceFst}\,V=\mathrm{sp}(V|_{\alpha})$ and $\mathrm{reduceSnd}\,V=\mathrm{sp}(V|_{\beta})$, the restrictions being taken along `heckeAlphaBar` and `heckeBetaBar`. The standing curve hypotheses are that $\bar F$ has principal divisors and is a curve over $\overline{\mathbb{Q}}$, and that $F_\kappa$ is a curve over $\kappa$ (principal divisors, residue fields finite over the base, module of Kähler differentials free of rank one). Finally $R$ is a `ProlongationTuple P`: a ring homomorphism `redBar` on $\kappa$ compatible with the residue map of $A$, a ring homomorphism $\iota : F_\kappa^{\mathrm{full}} \to F_\kappa$ acting coefficientwise by `redBar`, two regular prolongations $R_1,R_2$ of $A$ to $\bar F$ with residues in $F_\kappa^{\mathrm{full}}$, together with the identifications of their rings of integers with the localised modular rings, the relation of $R_2$ to $R_1$ through the Atkin–Lehner involution `atkinLehnerBar N q`, and the further normalisation clauses of that structure. The symbols `R.residue₁`, `R.residue₂` denote the residues attached to $R_1$, $R_2$ read in $F_\kappa$, and $\varphi:=$ `frobOnPlacesGeomLevel (ResidueField A) N data hKr` is the induced Frobenius map on places of $F_\kappa$. A place $v$ of $F_\kappa$ is affine, `IsAffineGeomPlace`, when both $j$ and $j_N$ lie in its valuation subring; `ssPlaces q N κ` is the set of places satisfying `IsSupersingularPlace q N κ`.
--
--   The hypotheses are as follows. (i) `hqN`: $q \nmid N$. (ii) `hR : R.IsModel`, the conjunction of the four laws `R.DivisorLawFst`, `R.DivisorLawSnd`, `R.CuspLawInfty`, `R.CuspLawZero`. (iii) `hO : R.OrderLawFixed`: for every $f\in\bar F$ lying in the integers of both $R_1$ and $R_2$ with both residues nonzero, every divisor $D$ with $D(V)=\mathrm{ord}_V f$ for all places $V$ of $\bar F$, and every place $v$ of $F_\kappa$ with $\varphi(\varphi(v))=v$ which is affine, the pushforward of $D$ along $\mathrm{reduceFst}$ takes at $v$ the value $\mathrm{ord}_v(\mathrm{residue}_1 f)+\mathrm{ord}_{\varphi(v)}(\mathrm{residue}_2 f)$. (iv) $W$ is a finite set of places of $F_\kappa$ and `hW` says that its members are exactly the supersingular places. (v) `hRL : R.RegularityLaw W`, whose two clauses require, for $f$ in both rings of integers: that at an affine place $v$ fixed by $\varphi^2$ at which $f$ has no pole on the first fibre, the first residue has nonnegative order at $v$ and the second residue nonnegative order at $\varphi(v)$ (whenever the respective residue is nonzero); and that for each node pair $s$ in `nodePairsOfPlaces (arithFrobC q (ResidueField A) N) W` at whose first component $f$ has no pole, there is a common value $c\in\kappa$ of the first residue at $s_1$ and of the second residue at $s_2$. Here `nodePairsOfPlaces` is the image of $W$ under the embedding `smulNodePairEmb` attached to the coefficientwise arithmetic Frobenius `arithFrobC q (ResidueField A) N`. (vi) `hNV : R.NodeValueLaw W`: for such $f$ with both residues nonzero and each node pair $s$ avoided by the divisor of $f$ (no place $V$ with $\mathrm{ord}_V f \ne 0$ reduces to $(s_1,s_2)$), the common value $c$ above may be taken nonzero. (vii) The cusp dictionary at level $N$: `hcusp`, every place $V$ of $\bar F$ whose first reduction is not affine is cuspidal, i.e. $\mathrm{ord}_V(j-a)\le 0$ for all $a\in A$; `hsplit`, every cuspidal $V$ satisfies `IsInftySide P V` or `IsZeroSide P V`; `hInftyNA` and `hInftyNA'`, for $V$ on the $\infty$-side both $\mathrm{reduceFst}\,V$ and $\mathrm{reduceSnd}\,V$ fail to be affine; `hZeroNA` and `hZeroNA'`, the same for $V$ on the $0$-side; `hφaff`, $\varphi$ carries affine places to affine places. (viii) `hNss`: both components of every node pair in `nodePairsOfPlaces (arithFrobC q (ResidueField A) N) W` are supersingular. (ix) `hzone`, a constructor of supersingularity-free zones: for every finite set $T'$ of non-supersingular places there is a finite set $Z'$ containing $T'$, containing $\varphi(v)$ for $v\in T'$, containing every $v$ with $\varphi(v)\in T'$, containing every non-affine place, containing every non-supersingular place fixed by $\varphi^2$, and consisting of non-supersingular places only. (x) `hAUX`, base points of both strict kinds in any quantity off any finite set: for every finite set $B$ of places of $F_\kappa$ and all $m_1,m_2\in\mathbb{N}$ there are families $Q_1:\mathrm{Fin}\,m_1\to$ places of $\bar F$ and $Q_2:\mathrm{Fin}\,m_2\to$ places of $\bar F$ such that every $Q_1(i)$ satisfies `P.IsStrictFst` (that is, $\varphi(\mathrm{reduceFst}\,Q_1(i))=\mathrm{reduceSnd}\,Q_1(i)$ and $\varphi^2(\mathrm{reduceFst}\,Q_1(i))\ne\mathrm{reduceFst}\,Q_1(i)$), every $Q_2(j)$ satisfies `P.IsStrictSnd` (that is, $\mathrm{reduceFst}\,Q_2(j)=\varphi(\mathrm{reduceSnd}\,Q_2(j))$ and $\varphi^2(\mathrm{reduceSnd}\,Q_2(j))\ne\mathrm{reduceSnd}\,Q_2(j)$), the maps $i\mapsto \mathrm{reduceFst}\,Q_1(i)$ and $j\mapsto\mathrm{reduceSnd}\,Q_2(j)$ are injective, and their values avoid $B$. (xi) `hINTERP`, interpolation on $F_\kappa$: for finite sets $U,Z_v,Z_a$ of places, a place $t_0$, $\beta\in\kappa$, a prescribed value function $\mathrm{val}$ on places with values in $\kappa$ and a finite set $\mathrm{bad}\subseteq\kappa$, assuming $U,Z_v,Z_a$ pairwise disjoint, $t_0$ in none of them, and $\#Z_v+2g+2\le \#U$ with $g=$ `genusFF (ResidueField A)` $F_\kappa$, there is $h$ in the Riemann–Roch space of $\sum_{u\in U}u$ with $\mathrm{ord}_{t_0}(h-\beta)=1$, with $h$ taking the value $\mathrm{val}(z)$ at each $z\in Z_v$, and at each $z\in Z_a$ some value $\gamma\notin\mathrm{bad}$. (xii) `hREALN`, realisation of a node-compatible pair of residues: for every effective divisor $D'$ on $\bar F$ with `P.IsGoodDiv D'` (each place in its support is strictly first or strictly second) such that the pushforward along $\mathrm{reduceFst}$ of the strictly-first part of $D'$ has degree at least $2g-1+\#W$ and the pushforward along $\mathrm{reduceSnd}$ of its strictly-second part has degree at least $2g-1$, and for every pair $g_1,g_2\in F_\kappa^{\mathrm{full}}$ with $\iota g_1$ in the Riemann–Roch space of the first pushforward, $\iota g_2$ in that of the second, and such that for every node pair $s$ there is $c\in\kappa$ with $\iota g_1$ taking the value $c$ at $s_1$ and $\iota g_2$ the value $c$ at $s_2$, there exists $G\in\bar F$ lying in the integers of $R_1$ and of $R_2$, lying in the Riemann–Roch space of $D'$, with $R_1$-residue $g_1$ and $R_2$-residue $g_2$. (xiii) A set-theoretic section of $\iota$: a function `toFull` $: F_\kappa \to F_\kappa^{\mathrm{full}}$ with $\iota(\mathrm{toFull}\,g)=g$ for all $g$. (xiv) Finally $T$ is a finite set of places of $F_\kappa$ none of which is supersingular, and $V_0$ is a place of $\bar F$ with $\mathrm{reduceFst}\,V_0\in T$ or $\mathrm{reduceSnd}\,V_0\in T$.
--
--   Under these hypotheses there exist a finite set $Z$ of places of $F_\kappa$, an element $f\in\bar F$ lying in the integers of $R_1$ and of $R_2$, and a divisor $D$ on $\bar F$ with the following properties. First, $Z$ is a zone: $T\subseteq Z$; every non-affine place of $F_\kappa$ lies in $Z$; every place $v$ with $\varphi(\varphi(v))=v$ which is not supersingular lies in $Z$; and no element of $Z$ is supersingular. Second, $f\ne 0$ and both residues `R.residue₁` and `R.residue₂` of $f$ are nonzero. Third, $D$ is the divisor of $f$, that is $D(V)=\mathrm{ord}_V f$ for every place $V$ of $\bar F$, and $D(V_0)=1$. Fourth, every place $V$ with $D(V)<0$ satisfies either `P.IsStrictFst V` together with $\mathrm{reduceFst}\,V\notin Z$, or `P.IsStrictSnd V` together with $\mathrm{reduceSnd}\,V\notin Z$. Fifth, every place $z$ lying in $Z$ or in $W$ and distinct from $\mathrm{reduceFst}\,V_0$ satisfies $\mathrm{ord}_z(\mathrm{residue}_1 f)=0$. Sixth, every place $z$ lying in $Z$ or in $W$ and distinct from $\mathrm{reduceSnd}\,V_0$ satisfies $\mathrm{ord}_z(\mathrm{residue}_2 f)=0$. Seventh, the order table at $V_0$ is one of the two alternatives: either $\mathrm{ord}_{\mathrm{reduceFst}V_0}(\mathrm{residue}_1 f)=1$ and $\mathrm{ord}_{\mathrm{reduceSnd}V_0}(\mathrm{residue}_2 f)=0$, or $\mathrm{ord}_{\mathrm{reduceFst}V_0}(\mathrm{residue}_1 f)=0$ and $\mathrm{ord}_{\mathrm{reduceSnd}V_0}(\mathrm{residue}_2 f)=1$. Eighth, there is no place $V\ne V_0$ with $D(V)\ge 1$ whose two reductions both lie in $Z$. Ninth, for every $V$ in the support of $D$ with $V\ne V_0$, neither $\mathrm{reduceFst}\,V$ nor $\mathrm{reduceSnd}\,V$ lies in $T$.
--
--   This is the core step of the one-point moving lemma at level $N$ on the glued special fibre of $X_0(Nq)$ in characteristic $q$: it produces a function on $X_0(Nq)$ over $\overline{\mathbb{Q}}$, integral for both prolongations, whose divisor has a simple zero at a prescribed place and whose two residues downstairs have prescribed order tables away from the chosen zone and the supersingular locus. All the geometric input (base points, interpolation, realisation, the cusp dictionary, zone construction) enters as explicit hypotheses; they are discharged in [`ModularCurve.PlaceSpecialization.ProlongationTuple.exists_ord_eq_one_forall_isStrict_reduceFst_reduceSnd_notMem`](thm.html#ModularCurve.PlaceSpecialization.ProlongationTuple.exists_ord_eq_one_forall_isStrict_reduceFst_reduceSnd_notMem), which is its only consumer.
